# A verb such as `ensure` or `means` takes either a noun (`ensure the
# order`) or a clause, a subject and its verb (`ensure the order
# holds`). Without `that`, the reader takes the noun as the verb's
# object and has to reread when the clause's own verb arrives. `that`
# marks the clause from its first word (`ensure that the order holds`).
#
# The rule counts the verb when a determiner follows it, a word that
# introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`. One to three more
# words come next, and then an auxiliary (a helping verb such as `is`,
# `has`, or `can`). A verb from a list such as `exists`, `holds`,
# `succeeded`, or `expires` counts in place of the auxiliary. The list
# leaves out `points`, `changes`, `starts`, and `returns`, because each
# also reads as a noun (`check the data points before the merge`). It
# also leaves out `changed` and `finished`, because after a noun they
# describe the noun (`check the files changed in the PR`).
#
# The words in between exclude `that`, `which`, `who`, `to`, pronouns,
# conjunctions, and commas, because each one ends the noun or opens a
# clause of its own (`check your docs to be sure`). The filter narrows
# the finding to the verb. `specify`, `see`, `states`, and `find` stay
# out, because a noun and a description of it follow them more often
# than a clause does.
#
# `this`, `that`, or `which` before a verb that ends in `s` skips the
# match, and so does `this also` or `this then`, because the
# broad-reference rule reports `this means`, `this also means`, `that
# means`, and `, which means`. The `that` guard also skips a `that` that
# points at the noun before it (`a type that guarantees the value stays
# put`), a miss the rule accepts.
#
# It hit 91 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 19 of 20 sampled hits
# dropped the `that` before a clause. The miss was a noun object before
# the main verb (`whether an error indicates a timeout can be
# ambiguous`).
expr imper [ensure, verify, confirm, (make sure), assume, check, remember, notice]
expr third [ensures, means, says, knows, shows, indicates, guarantees, expects, tells, assumes, implies, suggests, verifies, confirms]
expr aux [AUX, has, have, knows, needs, exists, runs, matches, contains, fails, holds, works, uses, succeeds, succeeded, expires, applies, belongs, stops, completes, finishes, reaches, appears, exceeds, equals]
expr w ![that, which, who, to, PRON, CCONJ, (,)]
expr det <[DET, your, its, their, our], !that>
expr rest @det [(@w @aux), (@w @w @aux), (@w @w @w @aux)]
expr main [<(@imper @rest), @imper>, <(![this, which, that, also, then] @third @rest), @third>, <(![this, that] [also, then] @third @rest), @third>]
let message "Missing `that`. Write `that` after the verb, so the noun after it does not read as its object."
let description "Flags `ensure`, `means`, and similar verbs that open a clause without `that`."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Ensure that the file exists."
allows "Check the file before the build."
allows "This means the pool is empty."
allows "This also means the pool is empty."
allows "Check your docs to be sure."
allows "Check the data points before the merge."
allows "Check the files changed in the PR."
